<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-distribution">
  <title>GitHub: Distribution</title>
<introduction>
  <p>
    In a classroom setting most of your files will typically be in private
    repositories to hold your code solutions for specific assigments for 
    your courses. However, since part of game development is distribution,
    you'll want a public repository where you can post versions of your games 
    that people can play.
  </p>
  <p>GitHub has an option to make pages from parts of your repository.
In addition, Godot can export your games to an html version that will
run directly on these pages. Examples of this can be found in
the <url href="https://wcu-cs-cooperlab.github.io/Fall2024GameDev/">
    Fall 2024 WCU Game Dev class</url> and the <url href="https://wcu-cs-cooperlab.github.io/Fall2026GameDev/">
        Fall 2026 WCU Game Dev class</url>
  </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-distribution-pages">
    <title>GitHub Pages</title>
    <p>
        The <url href="https://github.com/WCU-CS-CooperLab/GamePublish">GamePublish</url>
        repository is a template that can be used as a starting point for publishing. If you
        are part of a class, however, your instructor will have specific instructions
        for how to make a copy of the template so that it's set up for your class.
    </p>
    <p>
        Navigate to the GamePublish site and click on the Actions tab.
        Then click on the latest workflow run. You'll see a chart under
        jekyll.yml with a build block and a deploy block. Note that in the
        deploy block there is a url that links directly to home page of
        the published pages.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
